<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978)</h1><p class="st-meta">1973 CE – 1978 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The republic founded by Mohammad Daoud Khan after he overthrew his cousin King Zahir Shah, ended five years later by a communist coup.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 17 July 1973 the former prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a>, cousin and brother-in-law of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Zahir Shah</a>, seized power in a bloodless coup with the support of army officers, including members of the communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> faction. He abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-afghanistan">Kingdom of Afghanistan</a> and declared a republic with himself as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Daoud</a> ruled in an authoritarian style, pursuing the Pashtunistan dispute with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> and economic development with Soviet and Western aid. He suppressed Islamist opponents, many of whom fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and gradually pushed the communists out of government. A new constitution in 1977 created a one-party state, and he sought closer ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the Gulf states, angering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the murder of the communist leader Mir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> Khyber led to mass protests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Daoud</a> arrested communist leaders. On 27 April 1978 army officers loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party</a> killed Daoud and his family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a>, establishing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1973 CE – 1978 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:98.97%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1973 CE – 1978 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mohammad Daoud Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Daoud Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1976&amp;focus=republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978" aria-label="Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6757 1183M6757 1183L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=1976&amp;focus=republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
